-- title:
--   Assembling a frontier extension from child groups
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values. Let a frontier have nodes $p$, and for each $p$ let $c_p\ge1$ children with positive weights $w_{p,j}$ summing to $1$ and nonexpansive maps $g_{p,j}$ be given.
--
--   1. **Group value.** A local datum read at a global index through the concatenation bijection.
--   2. **Extension from groups.** The frontier extension whose children are the concatenated groups, with parent map, weights and maps read groupwise.
--   3. **Child averages.** At a common input $x$, the child average at $p$ is
--   $$
--   \sum_jw_{p,j}\,g_{p,j}(x),
--   $$
--   so local return estimates $\|\sum_jw_{p,j}g_{p,j}(x)-x\|<\tau$ hold for the assembled extension.
